## Main change

This PR moves the current main command `tbpore` to `tbpore process` and adds a new subcommand `tbpore cluster`. `tbpore cluster` first runs `psdm` on the given consensus sequences and then clusters the samples based on the `psdm` distance matrix and a given threshold, identically to what `H2H` does. @mbhall88 if you are able to review this on Tuesday it would be great, as we will send installation instructions to them on Tuesday, and it would be better if these pointed to the main branch of your repo (i.e. would be nice to have the main branch updated). But also it might be that you can't review on such a short notice, or you might find some issues that require fix before merging. In this case, we will just point them to a branch in my fork. Closes #25 

## Misc changes
* CLI changes:
	* `-h`, `-v`, `-V`, `-q` options moved to general usage options (i.e. these arguments are put between `tbpore` and the subcommand);
	* There are other arguments that we could potentially move to general usage options, as they are present in both subcommands... I just found it weird to specify them before the commands itself, e.g.:
	```
	tbpore --threads 8 --outdir results --cleanup --tmp tmpdir process <inputs>
	```
	I find it more natural to run:
	```
	tbpore process --threads 8 --outdir results --cleanup --tmp tmpdir <inputs>
	```
	Although we could move these 4 params to the general usage options if you prefer;
	See https://github.com/leoisl/tbpore/tree/cluster#usage for a detailed description of the current usage;
* Added `test-run` command to the `justfile`;
* Some general fixes to the `snakemake` pipeline;
* Some refactoring;


## Notes

* The [clustering testing code](https://github.com/leoisl/tbpore/blob/cluster/tests/test_clustering.py) has four test cases, with only one actually testing the core of the clustering code. This test is not a unit test, but a regression test that makes sure we produce the same clusters over and over again for a same `psdm` matrix (the one containing the distances for the 151 paper samples), and several clustering thresholds: `[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 10, 12, 15, 20, 50]`;
* `H2H` and `tbpore` have exact matches on the number and size of non-singleton clusters (see https://github.com/mbhall88/tbpore/issues/25#issuecomment-1127517506), but I did not check the cluster themselves. Should we do it?

Fixes #2051

The `RHI::FactoryManagerSystemComponent` had already the selection logic implemented. All that was missing was to populate the factories priority list.

In order to load the data into the member variable the user must define an entry in the settings registry using this path `/O3DE/Atom/RHI/FactoryManager/factoriesPriority`.

E.g. User specifies vulkan as top priority
```json
{
    "O3DE":
    {
        "Atom":
        {
            "RHI":
            {
                "FactoryManager":
                {
                    "factoriesPriority": [ "vulkan" ]
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
